  {
    "type": "updateEntity",
    "id": {
      "$bytes": "ce0b8161de7f4e09b3c873280fbeafb3"
    },
    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "a126ca530c8e48d5b88882c734c38935"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Saga pooled outcomes: methods and coverage",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## Pooled analyses of two trials\n\nThese findings combine the two Chicago randomized tutoring studies reported in the final AER article. They are additional analyses of those trials, not a third experiment. ITT estimates the effect of a tutoring offer; TOT uses randomized assignment as an instrument for participation. Alternate estimands and pooled results must not be counted as independent evidence.\n\n## Follow-up and uncertainty\n\nTable 5 reports the first postrandomization school year. Table 7 measures eleventh-grade outcomes in the year students would have reached that grade without retention, rather than one common elapsed follow-up interval. The authors describe persistent math effects but imprecise graduation effects. The terminal date for the outcome labeled Graduated ever is not yet established; it must not be interpreted as lifetime graduation. Table 7 retains duplicate randomization observations and clusters standard errors by individual.\n\n## Shared statistics and units\n\nThe control mean and estimated control-complier mean are different quantities. Reported FDR q-values belong to the source row and must not be relabeled as ordinary per-estimand p-values. Fraction-valued outcomes retain their original scale. The suspension label does not resolve whether its unit is days or events; leave its unit absent rather than inventing one.\n\n## Coverage\n\n[Final AER article](https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20210434), Tables 5 and 7, printed pages 751 and 753. Table 6's weighted per-year effect and upper bounds require separate modeling and are not included in these point-estimate tables. Study-specific later outcomes in the appendix remain separate required coverage.",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
